### Exemplary Independent Method Claim

1. **A computer-implemented method for estimating subsurface permeability within a geological region, the method comprising:**

   receiving seismic-velocity data representative of a plurality of subsurface locations within the geological region;

   identifying, based at least in part on the seismic-velocity data, a plurality of subsurface lithologies;

   obtaining at least one physical sample representative of each of the plurality of subsurface lithologies;

   for each physical sample, measuring:
   - a total porosity;
   - a permeability; and
   - an elastic seismic response at each of a plurality of applied stress states;

   partitioning the total porosity of each physical sample into a stiff-pore porosity component and a compliant-pore porosity component based at least in part on variation of the elastic seismic response with applied stress;

   calibrating, using the measured permeability and the stiff-pore and compliant-pore porosity components, at least:
   - a permeability model relating permeability to one or more pore-structure parameters; and
   - an elastic model relating seismic velocity to the stiff-pore porosity component, the compliant-pore porosity component, and stress;

   applying the calibrated elastic model to seismic-velocity data associated with additional subsurface locations to estimate respective stiff-pore and compliant-pore porosity components at the additional subsurface locations;

   applying the calibrated permeability model to the estimated stiff-pore and compliant-pore porosity components to determine respective permeability values for the additional subsurface locations; and

   generating a spatial representation of permeability across at least a portion of the geological region based on the determined permeability values.
